Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers on industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus's read_holding_registers/write_register return response objects with .isError() and .registers; pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk return 
    │   raw lists/booleans or None on failure, requiring changed error-checking logic; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RTU) communication; TCP 
    │   support is limited/different, and its API (read_register/write_register) has a different signature and error model (raises exceptions rather than 
    │   isError() checks) as noted in the file's docstring; The 'unit' keyword argument (unit id/slave address) differs in naming and semantics across these 
    │   libraries (e.g., 'slave' vs 'unit' vs constructor-level slave address); Connection lifecycle management (connect/close) differs: pyModbusTCP 
    │   auto-connects on first call, modbus-tk requires explicit RTU/TCP master setup, minimalmodbus binds a serial port at construction time
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide Modbus master/client functionality capable of reading and writing holding registers over a physical or network transport, 
        which is the sole function used here (ModbusIO wraps a client injected via dependency injection). pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk are TCP-focused 
        alternatives with comparable client APIs. minimalmodbus is explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a substitution target, though it uses a
        different read/write API and error-handling model (return values instead of response objects with .isError()), so it would require an adapter layer.

